**In-process bus — Spring `ApplicationEventPublisher` + `@TransactionalEventListener`:**

```java
// Publisher — domain code in the order service
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;
    private final OrderRepository orders;

    @Transactional
    public Order place(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
        Order o = orders.save(Order.from(cmd));
        events.publishEvent(new OrderPlaced(o.getId(), o.getCustomerId(), o.getTotal()));
        return o;
    }
}

// Subscriber 1 — same JVM, same transaction context
@Component
public class InventoryReservationListener {
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void on(OrderPlaced e) {
        // Runs in the same JVM after the OrderService transaction commits.
        // If THIS service crashes before this method runs, the event is GONE.
    }
}

// Subscriber 2 — same JVM
@Component
public class OrderMetricsListener {
    @EventListener
    public void on(OrderPlaced e) {
        Metrics.counter("orders.placed").increment();
    }
}
```

**Cross-process broker — Spring Kafka:**

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, OrderPlaced> kafka;
    private final OrderRepository orders;
    private final OutboxRepository outbox;

    @Transactional
    public Order place(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
        Order o = orders.save(Order.from(cmd));
        // Outbox: same DB transaction guarantees we don't lose the event on crash.
        outbox.save(OutboxEvent.of("OrderPlaced", o.getId(), serialize(o)));
        return o;
    }
}

// Subscribers can be in ANY service / language reachable to Kafka.
@Component
public class InventoryService {
    @KafkaListener(topics = "order-events", groupId = "inventory-svc")
    public void on(OrderPlaced e) {
        // Different process. Survives a crash of the order service.
    }
}
```

Both produce the same logical decoupling — the order service doesn't know who reacts to its events. The in-process version is two annotations and zero infrastructure; the broker version requires Kafka, an outbox table, a relay process, and a topic schema. **Use the in-process bus until the answer to 'who else needs to react to this event?' includes 'a different service'.**
